; The Atlas (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN P3.3): the roster's heavy gunship, and the slice's
; second frame per P3.10.
;
; hull 190 = the Kestrel's 100 scaled by the frame's own mass ratio (1.9x). That
; is P3.2's honest-mass doctrine applied to durability: a frame twice the tonnage
; is twice the airframe, and the number is DERIVED rather than fitted to make a
; P4.4 band come out. If the measurement says it is too much, that is a finding
; to report, not a number to quietly pre-tune.
;
; armor 3 is the frame's innate flat reduction — the trait P3.3 gives to exactly
; one frame. Sized against what the slice bestiary actually throws: a gnat sting
; is 7 (-> 4, a 43% cut), a raider bolt 8 (-> 5), a turret shell 10 (-> 7). Flat
; reduction is worth most against many small hits and least against few big ones,
; so ONE number reproduces P4.4's shape for the heavy column — great on gnat days,
; unimpressed by turrets — with nothing special-cased per enemy.
;
; evasion_style "hold" — the Atlas does not jink, and that is MEASURED (v1.81,
; three forced cells in one run): dodging changed nothing about what landed on it
; (0.11 taken either way) and cut its own gun from 0.11-0.17 down to 0.03. A
; 1.9x mass on softer rates cannot break a firing solution before it can no
; longer shoot back, so its survival is spent where it was bought — in hull 190
; and armor 3. The Kestrel keeps the tactical jink; see FrameConfig.evasion_style.
;
; component_armor rotor 0.006 — PROVISIONAL, and the SMALLEST value on the roster
; (Iteration 17 / E4.2, authored from role per E.q7). The plating ladder is
; denominated in raider bolts, because that is the round the game actually fires:
; a bolt strips 0.048 of rotor capability at the shipped severity, so 0.006 is an
; EIGHTH of a bolt and is deliberately a token.
;
; Why the armoured frame gets the least plating, which reads backwards and is not:
; the Atlas already ANSWERED its exposure, in the older grammar. Its 1.9x mass
; bought hull 190 and armor 3, and E4.2's own argument for plating is a mass
; argument — *"a 500 kg airframe can carry plating over its power bus and its
; gyro; a 650 g quad carries nothing"*. A 1.24 kg quad is much nearer the second
; sentence than the first, and its 0.28 m body gives it the roster's SMALLEST
; exposure (8.7% of raider rounds connect, against the Roc's 52%).
;
; What it is for, then: `armor` protects how long the Atlas lives, and nothing it
; carries protects how well it FLIES while living. A frame whose doctrine is to
; hold the line and eat the fire can be shot down to a wallowing wreck with its
; hull bar still healthy, so its designer spends a little of the remaining budget
; on the rotors and none of it anywhere else.
;
; MEASURED CONSEQUENCE, and it is the finding this value exists to expose
; (`armor_bench`): 0.006 turns away 34% of a raider bolt's rotor bite here, where
; the Roc's 0.024 — four times as much plating — turns away 50%. E4.3's separation
; splits a round on a 0.28 m airframe into one large share and two small ones, and
; flat plating eats the small ones ENTIRELY. Per round, plating is worth 2.71x more
; on this frame than on a Roc. Per FIGHT it inverts, because the Roc is hit six
; times as often; see armor_bench table D.
[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="FrameConfig" load_steps=3 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://resources/frame_config.gd" id="1_frame"]
[ext_resource type="Resource" path="res://resources/default_flight_atlas.tres" id="2_flight"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_frame")
frame_id = &"atlas"
display_name = "Atlas"
flight_config = ExtResource("2_flight")
hull = 190.0
armor = 3.0
component_armor = {
&"rotor": 0.006
}
evasion_style = &"hold"
